WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.967
Standardization.

2
00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:06.733
It puts every feature on the same footing, but only if you do it in the right order.

3
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:12.400
Standardization rescales each feature to zero mean and unit variance.

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.767
Subtract the mean, and the feature is centered on zero.

5
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:19.333
Divide by the standard deviation, so its spread is one.

6
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:21.967
Now every feature gets an equal vote.

7
00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:24.400
Here is the formula.

8
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:25.933
We subtract the mean.

9
00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:28.333
And divide by the standard deviation.

10
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:32.963
Unlike min-max, the result is unbounded, but not outlier-proof.

11
00:00:32.963 --> 00:00:39.767
One extreme value still inflates the mean and spread, so for outliers use a robust scaler or a log.

12
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:41.636
Why bother?

13
00:00:41.636 --> 00:00:44.433
Think about K nearest neighbors on exoplanets.

14
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:49.234
Temperature is in the thousands, while radius is a single digit number.

15
00:00:49.234 --> 00:00:55.067
About ninety nine percent of the distance is temperature, so radius might as well not exist.

16
00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:59.367
Now the rule that separates professionals from tutorials.

17
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:00.967
Take the training data.

18
00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:05.300
Fit the scaler on it alone, learning its mean and standard deviation.

19
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.633
Apply those numbers to the training set.

20
00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:10.969
And reuse the very same numbers on the test set.

21
00:01:10.969 --> 00:01:12.233
Never refit on test.

22
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:14.767
Here is the safe way.

23
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:18.333
We put the scaler and the model together in one pipeline.

24
00:01:18.633 --> 00:01:25.033
Cross validation then refits the scaler on each training fold only, so the test folds never leak in.

25
00:01:26.233 --> 00:01:27.700
Back to our exoplanets.

26
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.600
Temperature runs in the thousands, radius mostly from half an Earth to fifteen.

27
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.133
Wrap a standard scaler into the pipeline.

28
00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:41.233
K nearest neighbors goes from coin flip to competitive, because every feature now votes equally.

29
00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:46.099
The classic mistake is scaling everything before you split.

30
00:01:46.099 --> 00:01:51.567
The test set's statistics leak into training, and your evaluation is quietly optimistic.

31
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:56.400
The fix is a pipeline, which enforces fit on train only, automatically.

32
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.406
Let's recap.

33
00:01:58.406 --> 00:02:01.767
Standardization gives zero mean and unit variance.

34
00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:04.933
Distance based and gradient based models need it.

35
00:02:05.233 --> 00:02:06.933
Fit on the training data only.

36
00:02:07.233 --> 00:02:09.528
And use a pipeline to guarantee it.

37
00:02:09.528 --> 00:02:11.233
Next up: one-hot encoding.
